Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever the daily summary shows an order count, it counts every order in the list, including store-credit orders. On a day with no orders it shows 0 orders.
  2. Whenever the daily summary shows revenue, revenue is the sum, over card orders only, of each card order's item total after the discount code plus its shipping charge. Store-credit orders add nothing to revenue. On a day with no orders, revenue is Rs 0.
  3. Whenever the daily summary shows the average, it is revenue divided by the number of card orders, rounded down to whole rupees (for example Rs 1,000 over 3 card orders is shown as Rs 333). If there are no card orders, the average is Rs 0.
  4. Whenever the daily summary meets a cancelled or unpaid order, the software must not decide how to treat it. That choice is left to the owner, because his policy sets no rule for it.
  5. Whenever the daily summary is calculated, tax is undefined. The software must not silently include it or exclude it. The owner will decide how tax is treated.
  6. Whenever refunds could affect the daily summary, the software must not decide whether or how refunds are taken off the summary. The owner will decide that separately.
  7. Whenever a customer earns loyalty points on an order, she earns 1 point for every full Rs 100 of the items total after the discount (for example Rs 1,250 earns 12 points). This applies only if the items total after the discount is Rs 500 or more and the order is not paid with store credit. Otherwise she earns no points. Final-sale orders earn double points. Shipping earns nothing.
  8. Whenever an order is fully refunded, the system leaves the loyalty points untouched. It does not deduct them, does not keep them on purpose, and does not decide anything about them. The owner will decide about the points himself when it happens.
  9. Whenever a refund is requested on an order that has already been fully refunded, refuse the request.
  10. Whenever a refund request gives no reason, or gives a reason that is not one of the listed refund reasons (for example 'my horoscope said no'), treat it as a changed-mind return. Only an actual 'damaged' reason is treated as damaged.
  11. Whenever goods are returned and are not damaged (changed-mind return), count the days from the day of delivery (delivered 1 March and requested 10 March is day 10). If the request is within 14 days of delivery, refund the full amount paid for the returned items. From day 15 to day 30, refund half of that amount. After day 30, refund nothing.
  12. Whenever goods are reported as damaged, count the days from the day of delivery. If the report is within 90 days of delivery, refund the full amount paid for the items plus the shipping fee, even if it is after the normal return period (for example, a report on day 20 gets a full refund). After 90 days, refund nothing.
  13. Whenever a refund is calculated, the shipping fee is refunded only for damaged goods. All other refunds are for the items only, without the shipping fee.
  14. Whenever a customer returns all items of an order, the amount the refund is based on is the amount actually paid for the items after subtracting the discount code (for example Rs 4,500 on a Rs 5,000 order paid with a Rs 500 coupon). The timing and damaged rules then decide how much of it is refunded.
  15. Whenever a customer returns only some of the items in an order, the amount the refund is based on is, for each returned item, its price minus that item's share of the discount code. The timing and damaged rules then decide how much of it is refunded.
  16. Whenever a refund is issued, for any reason, it goes back the same way the order was paid. Card orders are refunded to the original card, and store-credit orders are refunded as store credit.
